  "query_preview": "..\nThursday night through Monday: Thursday night will be another \nchilly night with light winds and mostly clear skies. Be \nprepared to protect sensitive plants once again with \ntemperatures falling below freezing regionwide. Dry conditions \nwill close out the work week and last through the weekend as a \nridge of high pressure builds over the region. In addition to \ndry conditions, the ridge will deliver light winds, warming \ntemperatures, and clear to partly cloudy skies. \n\nAn area of low pressure enters the picture by late weekend into\nearly next week. This system drops out of the Gulf of AK Sunday\ninto Monday and follows the coastline down to the\nOregon/California border. Mid to high level cloud cover will \nincrease, but mainly dry conditions will continue for the Inland\nNW through Sunday and Monday with persistent easterly winds.\nTemperatures will warm by 4 to 8 degrees each day starting today\n(Thursday) through Monday. By Monday afternoon, high\ntemperatures will range from the mid 70s to the low 80s, nearly\n20 degrees above seasonal norms for mid to late April. \nOvernight lows, however, will stay cool in the mid 30s to low\n40s under the light winds and clear skies. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday: Temperatures will start trending back\ntoward seasonal norms by midweek as the offshore low moves\ninland and winds shift out of the southwest. There is still \nsome variation within ensembles as to when exactly the low will \nmove onshore and what track it will follow, but regardless, \nprecipitation amounts are looking light and spotty, as is \ntypical with convective spring showers. /Fewkes",
